<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.3.1: Determining the Number of Solutions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02123</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.3.1: Determining the Number of Solutions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a2f460f9-3e2f-45db-b804-983d9ec4cba6</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<para>How many solutions does each equation have? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(x^2 = 9\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the number of solutions for this equation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Two solutions</para>
    
    <para>Both \(3^2\) and \((−3)^2\) equal 9.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(x^2 = 0\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the number of solutions for this equation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>One solution</para>
    
    <para>Only \(0^2\) is 0.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(x^2 − 1 = 3\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the number of solutions for this equation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Two solutions</para>
    
    <para>Add 1 to both sides of the equation. Then, both \(2^2\) and \((−2)^2\) equal 4.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(2x^2= 50\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the number of solutions for this equation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Two solutions</para>
    
    <para>Divide both sides of the equation by 2. Then, both \(5^2\) and \((−5)^2\) equal 25.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\((x + 1)(x + 1) = 0\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the number of solutions for this equation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>One solution</para>
    
    <para>Setting each factor equal to zero results in −1 being the only solution.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(x(x − 6) = 0\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the number of solutions for this equation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Two solutions</para>
    
    <para>Setting each factor equal to zero results in 0 and 6 being solutions.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\((x − 1)(x − 1) = 4\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the number of solutions for this equation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para> Two solutions</para>
    
    <para>By trial and error, both 3 and −1 make the equation true.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How are the solutions to questions 2 and 5 different from the other solutions?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your explanation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Questions 2 and 5 only have one solution each, while the others have two solutions.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>A <term class="glossary-tooltip">double root</term> is a root that appears twice in the solution of an algebraic equation.</para></section>
</content>
</document>
